Take the reference photograph and change ONE thing: the empty blue chairs at the back of the classroom are now taken by three more adult students of an evening Spanish class for beginners, seated at the back tables, each with an open notebook in front of them.

The three new students, each clearly their own person:
at the back table by the window, a broad-shouldered man in his fifties with a shaved head and a short black beard, in a dark-blue shirt, writing in his notebook;
beside him, a young woman of about twenty with long straight black hair, in a mustard-yellow hoodie, smiling towards the teacher;
at the back table nearer the door, a man in his thirties with short ginger hair and a ginger beard, in a dark-green jacket, his chin resting on his hand.

Everything else stays exactly as it is in the reference photograph: the same camera position, lens and framing; the teacher, the young man in the blue sweater, the young woman in the oatmeal sweater and the woman in the red dress in the same places, with the same faces, clothes, expressions and poses; the same desk, laptop, smartphone, mug and cable; the same chalkboard, clock, door with its two coats, framed photograph, printer, window, radiator, bookshelf, plant and lamp. The radiator, the coats on the door and the navy backpack under the table stay in full view.
